The [[Genesee River]] flows northward from its source in northern Pennsylvania to enter Lake Ontario at [[Rochester, Monroe County, New York|Rochester]], [[New York (state)|New York]].

[[Image:Genesee river.jpg|thumb|350px|right| Genesee River Middle Section Near Mount Morris Showing Glacial Changes in Valleys]] 

The present river valley has been modified extensively from [[Ice age|preglacial]] river valleys. A lobe of the last glacier ([[Wisconsin glaciation|Wisconsonian]]) pushed southward almost to the [[Pennsylvania]] line, dramatically reshaping the drainage patterns of central and western [[New York (state)|New York]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Young |first=Richard A. |last2=Gordon |first2=Lee M. |last3=Owen |first3=Lewis A. |last4=Huot |first4=Sebastien |last5=Zerfas |first5=Timothy D. |date=2020 |title=Evidence for a late glacial advance near the beginning of the Younger Dryas in western New York State: An event postdating the record for local Laurentide ice sheet recession |url=https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/evidence-for-a-late-glacial-advance-near-the-beginning-of-the-you |journal=Geosphere |volume=17 |pages=1–35 |doi=10.1130/GES02257.1 |issn=1553-040X|doi-access=free }}</ref>

==Middle Genesee==

The first image shows the evidence of the glacial rerouting for the middle section of the Genesee. The original river had two branches. The east branch runs southeast of Mount Morris. It was much larger than the west branch, flowing from near present-day [[Canisteo (town), New York|Canisteo, New York]] to empty into the preglacial Ontario River (now lake) north of Rochester. The headwaters of the east branch were blocked and diverted southward into the present [[Susquehanna River]] system by huge glacial terminal [[moraine]]s just south of [[Dansville, Livingston County, New York|Dansville]]. The entire old valley was buried in that area. This left only the small [[Canaseraga Creek]], to flow in the remnant of the large ancient east branch valley from Dansville to Mount Morris.

The current upper Genesee (above Mount Morris) was only the west branch in preglacial times, a tributary river to the original. This branch, which entered the old valley was also dammed in the [[Nunda (town), New York|Nunda]] area by moraines. A lake was formed with [[varve]] [[clay]] sediments visible along the valley sides in many spots, but the lake drained as the river found a way to cut a new valley northeastward from [[Portageville, New York|Portageville]] a little west of the original valley, then drop back into the old valley at Mount Morris. This section of new valley is a spectacular gorge with three scenic [[waterfall]]s, that is now the site of [[Letchworth State Park]].

Portions of other preglacial valleys can be seen both east and west of the Genesee, occupied now by [[lake]]s. The remnants of these valleys were buried by glacial drift. A [[drumlin]] field can be seen just north of [[Finger Lakes|Conesus and Hemlock Lakes]].

